on by 9-demethylretinal. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>118</volume>:<fpage>377</fpage>&#x02013;390.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11585850</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib55"><citation citation-type="journal">Matthews, H.R., G.L. Fain, R.L.W. Murphy, and T.D. Lamb. <year>1990</year>. Light adaptation in cone photoreceptors of the salamander: a role for cytoplasmic calcium. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>420</volume>:<fpage>447</fpage>&#x02013;469.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2109062</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib56"><citation citation-type="journal">Matthews, H.R., R.L.W. Murphy, G.L. Fain, and T.D. Lamb. <year>1988</year>. Photoreceptor light adaptation is mediated by cytoplasmic calcium concentration. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>334</volume>:<fpage>67</fpage>&#x02013;69.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2455234</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib57"><citation citation-type="journal">McCarthy, S.T., J.P. Younger, and W.G. Owen. <year>1996</year>. Dynamic, spatially nonuniform calcium regulation in frog rods exposed to light. <source>J. Neurophysiol.</source> <volume>76</volume>:<fpage>1991</fpage>&#x02013;2004.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8890309</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib58"><citation citation-type="journal">Meyertholen, E.P., M.J. Wilson, and S.E. Ostroy. <year>1986</year>. The effects of HEPES, bicarbonate and calcium on the cGMP content of vertebrate rod photoreceptors and the isolated electrophysiological effects of cGMP and calcium. <source>Vision Res.</source> <volume>26</volume>:<fpage>521</fpage>&#x02013;533.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3016996</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib59"><citation citation-type="journal">Murnick, J.G., and T.D. Lamb. <year>1996</year>. Kinetics of desensitization induced by saturating flashes in toad and salamander rods. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>495</volume>:<fpage>1</fpage>&#x02013;13.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8866347</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib60"><citation citation-type="journal">Nakatani, K., Y. Koutalos, and K.-W. Yau. <year>1995</year>. Ca<sup>2+</sup> modulation of the cGMP-gated channel of bullfrog retinal rod photoreceptors. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>484</volume>:<fpage>69</fpage>&#x02013;76.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7541463</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib61"><citation citation-type="journal">Nakatani, K., and K.-W. Yau. <year>1988</year>. Calcium and light adaptation in retinal rods and cones. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>334</volume>:<fpage>69</fpage>&#x02013;71.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3386743</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib62"><citation citation-type="journal">Nikonov, S., N. Engheta, and E.N. Pugh, Jr. <year>1998</year>. Kinetics of recovery of the dark-adapted salamander rod photoresponse. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>111</volume>:<fpage>7</fpage>&#x02013;37.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9417132</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib63"><citation citation-type="journal">Nikonov, S., T.D. Lamb, and E.N. Pugh, Jr. <year>2000</year>. The role of steady phosphodiesterase activity in the kinetics and sensitivity of the light-adapted salamander rod photoresponse. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>116</volume>:<fpage>795</fpage>&#x02013;824.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11099349</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib64"><citation citation-type="journal">Palczewski, K., I. Subbaraya, W.A. Gorczyca, B.S. Helekar, C.C. Ruiz, H. Ohguro, J. Huang, X. Zhao, J.W. Crabb, R.S. Johnson, et al. <year>1994</year>. Molecular cloning and characterization of retinal photoreceptor guanylyl cyclase-activating protein. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>13</volume>:<fpage>395</fpage>&#x02013;404.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7520254</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib65"><citation citation-type="journal">Pepperberg, D.R., M.C. Cornwall, M. Kahlert, K.P. Hofmann, J. Jin, G.J. Jones, and H. Ripps. <year>1992</year>. Light-dependent delay in the falling phase of the retinal rod photoresponse. <source>Vis. Neurosci.</source> <volume>8</volume>:<fpage>9</fpage>&#x02013;18.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1739680</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib66"><citation citation-type="journal">Pepperberg, D.R., J. Jin, and G.J. Jones. <year>1994</year>. Modulation of transduction gain in light adaptation of retinal rods. <source>Vis. Neurosci.</source> <volume>11</volume>:<fpage>53</fpage>&#x02013;62.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8011583</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib67"><citation citation-type="other">Pugh, E.N., Jr., and T.D. Lamb. 2000. Phototransduction in vertebrate rods and cones: molecular mechanisms of amplification, recovery and light adaptation. Handbook of Biological Physics: Molecular Mechanisms in Visual Transduction. vol. 3. D.G. Stavenga, W.J. DeGrip, and E.N. Pugh, Jr., editors. Elsevier Science, Amsterdam. 183&#x02013;255.</citation></ref><ref id="bib68"><citation citation-type="journal">Pugh, E.N., Jr., S. Nikonov, and T.D. Lamb. <year>1999</year>. Molecular mechanisms of vertebrate photoreceptor light adaptation. <source>Curr. Opin. Neurobiol.</source> <volume>9</volume>:<fpage>410</fpage>&#x02013;418.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10448166</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib69"><citation citation-type="journal">Reuter, T.E., R.H. White, and G. Wald. <year>1971</year>. Rhodopsin and porphyropsin fields in the adult bullfrog retina. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>58</volume>:<fpage>351</fpage>&#x02013;371.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5315587</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib70"><citation citation-type="journal">Rieke, F., and D.A. Baylor. <year>1996</year>. Molecular origin of continuous dark noise in rod photoreceptors. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>71</volume>:<fpage>2553</fpage>&#x02013;2572.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8913594</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib71"><citation citation-type="journal">Sagoo, M.S., and L. Lagnado. <year>1996</year>. The action of cytoplasmic calcium on the cGMP-activated channel in salamander rod photoreceptors. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>497</volume>:<fpage>309</fpage>&#x02013;319.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8961177</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib72"><citation citation-type="journal">Tamura, T., K. Nakatani, and K.-W. Yau. <year>1991</year>. Calcium feedback and sensitivity regulation in primate rods. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>98</volume>:<fpage>95</fpage>&#x02013;130.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1719127</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib73"><citation citation-type="journal">Tsuboi, S., H. Matsumoto, and A. Yamazaki. <year>1994</year>. Phosphorylation of an inhibitory subunit of cGMP phosphodiesterase in <italic>Rana catesbeiana</italic> rod photoreceptors. II. A possible mechanism for the turnoff of cGMP phosphodiesterase without GTP hydrolysis. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>269</volume>:<fpage>15024</fpage>&#x02013;15029.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8195139</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib74"><citation citation-type="journal">Xu, L.X., Y. Tanaka, V.A. Bonderenko, I. Matsuura, H. Matsumoto, A. Yamazaki, and F. Hayashi. <year>1998</year>. Phosphorylation of the gamma subunit of the retinal photoreceptor cGMP phosphodiesterase by the cAMP-dependent protein kinase and its effect on the gamma subunit interaction with other proteins. <source>Biochemistry.</source> <volume>37</volume>:<fpage>6205</fpage>&#x02013;6213.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9558360</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib75"><citation citation-type="journal">Yamazaki, A., V.A. Bondarenko, S. Dua, M. Yamazaki, J. Usukura, and F. Hayashi. <year>1996</year>. Possible stimulation of retinal rod recovery to dark state by cGMP release from a cGMP phosphodiesterase noncatalytic site. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>271</volume>:<fpage>32495</fpage>&#x02013;32498.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8955069</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib76"><citation citation-type="journal">Yau, K.-W., and K. Nakatani. <year>1984</year>. Electrogenic Na-Ca exchange in retinal rod outer segment. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>311</volume>:<fpage>661</fpage>&#x02013;663.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6434995</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="fn1"><label>*</label><p><italic>Abbreviation used in this paper:</italic> ROS, rod outer segment.</p></fn></fn-group><app-group><app id="app1"><title>APPENDIX</title><p>Here, our goal is to show how the extent of light adaptation may be extracted from a series of responses to steps of light and to express it in terms of feedback regulation of phototransduction cascade components. We begin by formally describing the rod response to a step of light in the absence of feedback reactions (the &#x0201c;no adaptation&#x0201d; case&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn1">Eq. 1</xref>). We then explain how the no adaptation response can be predicted based on experimental data obtained with operational feedbacks. Finally, we show how the different parameters of the phototransduction cascade could contribute to adaptation. Many of the equations appearing in this Appendix have been obtained previously (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib36">Hodgkin and Nunn, 1988</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib30">Forti et al., 1989</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib55">Matthews et al., 1990</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib72">Tamura et al., 1991</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib43">Koutalos et al., 1995b</xref>). For comparative purposes, we shall use the most recent notation and equations developed by <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib63">Nikonov et al. (2000)</xref> wherever possible. Henceforth, their derivation will be referred to as NLP.</p><p>The concentration of cGMP, the molecule that controls the light-sensitive conductance of the ROS plasma membrane, is set by the balance between its synthesis by guanylate cyclase <italic>&#x003b1;</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) and its hydrolysis by PDE <italic>&#x003b2;</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib36">Hodgkin and Nunn, 1988</xref>): <disp-formula id="eqn5"><label>(A1)</label><tex-math id="M6">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\begin{matrix}\frac{dcG \left \left(t\right) \right }{dt}={\mathrm{{\alpha}}} \left \left(t\right) \right -cG{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\beta}}} \left \left(t\right) \right \\ ={\mathrm{{\alpha}}} \left \left(t\right) \right -cG{\cdot} \left \left({\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{d}+{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{s} \left \left(t\right) \right \right) \right {\mathrm{,}}\end{matrix}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <italic>&#x003b1;</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) and <italic>&#x003b2;</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) are expressed per unit volume of the ROS cytoplasm and <italic>&#x003b2;</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) is the sum of the dark PDE activity <italic>&#x003b2;<sub>d</sub></italic> and the light-stimulated PDE activity <italic>&#x003b2;<sub>s</sub></italic>(t). Our <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn5">Eq. A1</xref> is essentially NLP's <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn7">Eq. A3</xref>. For completeness, the exchange of free cGMP with that bound to noncatalytic sites on PDE and to cGMP-gated channels should be included in <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn5">Eq. A1</xref>. However, the rate of release of cGMP from PDE sites is low compared with the guanylate cyclase activity, and cannot contribute significantly to the balance of free cGMP. For instance, the saturating background light producing 46,500 R* s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup> results in the release of cGMP from PDE sites at the rate of 0.4% of its dark content per second, or &#x0223c;0.8 &#x003bc;M s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup> (<xref rid="fig5" ref-type="fig">Fig. 5</xref>). At the same time, the saturating background results in a cGMP flux of 12&#x02013;30 &#x003bc;M s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup> (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib13">Calvert et al., 1998</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib63">Nikonov et al., 2000</xref>), but which may be as high as 100 &#x003bc;M s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup> (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib21">Dawis et al., 1988</xref>). The total amount of cGMP bound to the channels is simply too low to have any effect. Thus, we neglect the release of cGMP bound to PDE noncatalytic sites and to the channel from further consideration of dcG(t)/dt.</p><p>At the peak and the plateau of the steady response, <disp-formula><tex-math id="M7">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{dcG \left \left(t\right) \right }{dt}=0{\mathrm{,}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>and during the slow phase of adaptation, <disp-formula><tex-math id="M8">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{dcG \left \left(t\right) \right }{dt}{\mathrm{{\ll}{\alpha}}} \left \left(t\right) \right ,cG{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\beta}}} \left \left(t\right) \right {\mathrm{.}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>Therefore, <disp-formula id="eqn6"><label>(A2)</label><tex-math id="M9">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}cG \left \left(t\right) \right \approx \frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}} \left \left(t\right) \right }{{\mathrm{{\beta}}} \left \left(t\right) \right }{\mathrm{.}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>The quasi&#x02013;steady-state <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn6">Eq. A2</xref> can be applied to times from the peak of the response onward. It is not applicable to the steeply rising front of the response where the complete differential <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn5">Eq. A1</xref> should be solved.</p><p>The cGMP hydrolytic activity elicited by a light step of intensity <italic>I</italic> (measured in photons &#x003bc;m<sup>&#x02212;2</sup> s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>) in the absence of feedback is <disp-formula id="eqn7"><label>(A3)</label><tex-math id="M10">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{s0} \left \left(t\right) \right =I{\cdot}A_{c}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{{\mathrm{{\infty}}}0}{\cdot}f_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right {\mathrm{,}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where the subscript 0 denotes the value in the absence of light adaptation, <disp-formula id="eqn8"><label>(A4)</label><tex-math id="M11">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{{\mathrm{{\infty}}}}=\frac{{\mathrm{{\nu}}}_{RE}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{sub}}{k_{R}{\cdot}k_{E}}={\mathrm{{\nu}}}_{RE}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\tau}}}_{R}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\tau}}}_{E}{\cdot}\frac{0.5 \left \left({k_{cat}}/{K_{m}}\right) \right }{N_{AV}{\cdot}V_{cyto}}{\mathrm{,}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula><disp-formula id="eqn9"><label>(A5)</label><tex-math id="M12">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}f \left \left(t\right) \right =1-\frac{k_{R}{\cdot}{\mathrm{exp}} \left \left(-k_{E}{\cdot}t\right) \right -k_{E}{\cdot}{\mathrm{exp}} \left \left(-k_{R}{\cdot}t\right) \right }{k_{R}-k_{E}}{\mathrm{.}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>Here &#x003b2;<sub>&#x0221e;</sub> denotes the final steady level of hydrolytic activity elicited by continuous light producing 1 R*s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>, <italic>A</italic><sub>c</sub> is the ROS light collecting area (measured in &#x003bc;m<sup>2</sup>), <italic>&#x003bd;<sub>RE</sub></italic> is the steady-state rate of PDE activation in a rod produced by one R* s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>, <italic>k</italic><sub>R</sub> (=1/<italic>&#x003c4;<sub>R</sub></italic>) is the rate constant of rhodopsin quenching, <italic>k</italic><sub>E</sub> (=1/<italic>&#x003c4;<sub>E</sub></italic>) is the rate constant of PDE* shutoff, <italic>&#x003b2;<sub>sub</sub></italic> is the rate constant of cGMP hydrolysis by a single PDE* catalytic subunit of which there are two per enzyme unit (compare with NLP's <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn12">Eq. A8</xref>), <italic>k</italic><sub>cat</sub> is the maximum rate of cGMP hydrolysis by a PDE enzyme unit, <italic>K</italic><sub>m</sub> is the PDE Michaelis constant, <italic>N</italic><sub>AV</sub> is Avogadro's number, and <italic>V</italic><sub>cyto</sub> is the ROS cytoplasmic volume. <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn7">Eqs. A3</xref> and <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn9">A5</xref> were obtained from the integration of NLP's <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn5">Eqs. A1</xref> and <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn6">A2</xref>. <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn8">Eq. A4</xref> follows from NLP's <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn15">Eq. A11</xref>. <italic>f(t)</italic> describes the PDE* activity, relative to that at steady-state, as a function of time after light onset.</p><p>Relating step-induced PDE activity to the circulating current flowing into the ROS, <italic>i</italic> : <disp-formula id="eqn10"><label>(A6)</label><tex-math id="M13">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\begin{matrix}i\approx i_{m}{\cdot} \left \left(\frac{cG}{K_{cG}}\right) \right ^{n_{cG}}\\ =i_{m}{\cdot} \left \left[\frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}}{K_{cG} \left \left({\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{d}+{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{s} \left \left(t\right) \right \right) \right }\right] \right ^{n_{cG}}{\mathrm{,}}\end{matrix}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <italic>i</italic><sub>m</sub> is the maximum possible current when all channels are open, <italic>K</italic><sub>cG</sub> is the dissociation constant of cGMP from the channels, and <italic>n</italic><sub>cG</sub> is Hill's coefficient (NLP, <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn1">Eq. 1</xref>). In the absence of any evidence to the contrary, we assume that the cooperativity of cGMP binding to the channel is unaffected by light adaptation, i.e., <italic>n</italic><sub>cG</sub> = <italic>n</italic><sub>cG0</sub>. However, <italic>&#x003b1;</italic> and <italic>K</italic><sub>cG</sub> are subject to feedback during light adaptation. The magnitude of the current flowing in darkness (<italic>i</italic><sub>d</sub>) is <disp-formula id="eqn11"><label>(A7)</label><tex-math id="M14">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}i_{d}\approx i_{m} \left \left(\frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{0}}{K_{cG_{0}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{d}}\right) \right ^{n_{cG}}{\mathrm{,}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>and the fractional circulating current after light stimulation is the ratio of <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn10">Eq. A6</xref> to <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn11">Eq. A7</xref>, <disp-formula id="eqn12"><label>(A8)</label><tex-math id="M15">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{i \left \left(t\right) \right }{i_{d}}\approx  \left \left(\frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}}{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{0}}{\cdot}\frac{K_{cG_{0}}}{K_{cG}}{\cdot}\frac{1}{1+{ \left \left({\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{s} \left \left(t\right) \right \right) \right }/{{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{d}}}\right) \right ^{n_{cG}}{\mathrm{.}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>In the absence of light adaptation, the fractional response to a given step intensity <italic>I</italic> is <disp-formula id="eqn13"><label>(A9)</label><tex-math id="M16">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{r_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right }{r_{max}}=1-\frac{i_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right }{i_{d}}=1- \left \left(\frac{1}{1+{{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{s_{0}} \left \left(t\right) \right }/{{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{d}}}\right) \right ^{n_{cG}}{\mathrm{.}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>Note that <italic>I</italic> is a part of <italic>&#x003b2;<sub>s</sub></italic><sub>0</sub>(<italic>t</italic>) (<xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn7">Eq. A3</xref>). <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn13">Eq. A9</xref> can be rewritten as <disp-formula id="eqn14"><label>(A10)</label><tex-math id="M17">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{r_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right }{r_{max}}=1- \left \left(\frac{1}{1+I{\cdot}F \left \left(t\right) \right }\right) \right ^{n_{cG}}{\mathrm{,}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <italic>F</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) is a sensitivity factor, <disp-formula id="eqn15"><label>(A11)</label><tex-math id="M18">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}F \left \left(t\right) \right =A_{c}\frac{{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{{\mathrm{{\infty}}}0}}{{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{d}}{\cdot}f_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right {\mathrm{,}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>expressing the fractional degree of PDE activation by a step of light of unit intensity. <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn14">Eq. A10</xref> is identical to <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn1">Eq. 1</xref>. Equations of this form have been derived previously (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib30">Forti et al., 1989</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib55">Matthews et al., 1990</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib72">Tamura et al., 1991</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib43">Koutalos et al., 1995b</xref>).</p><p>In principle, the relation between <italic>r</italic><sub>0</sub>(<italic>t</italic>)/<italic>r</italic><sub>max</sub> and <italic>I</italic> may be found experimentally from physiological recordings during inhibition of Ca<sup>2+</sup> feedback reactions. Unfortunately, direct determination of <italic>F</italic> in our experiments was not possible because of technical problems in inhibiting Ca<sup>2+</sup> decline in frog rods. The ciliary connection between the inner and outer segments in frog rods is quite fragile and tended to break when exposed to the flow of feedback-inhibiting solution. Moreover, responses of isolated rods were distorted by the small current carrying capacity of the inner segment, which was largely saturated by the dark current level. Yet, we selected bullfrogs for these experiments expressly to compare the physiology with the well characterized biochemistry in general, and more specifically, with the kinetics of cGMP dissociation from the noncatalytic binding sites on PDE in intact rods. Therefore, an alternative method to find <italic>F</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) was used.</p><p>In the linear response range (<italic>r</italic><sub>0</sub>(<italic>t</italic>)<italic>/r</italic><sub>max</sub> &#x0226a; 1), <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn14">Eq. A10</xref> becomes <disp-formula id="eqn16"><label>(A12)</label><tex-math id="M19">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{r_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right }{r_{max}}=n_{cG}{\cdot}I{\cdot}F \left \left(t\right) \right {\mathrm{.}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>On the other hand, the step response is equal to the product of the light intensity and the time integral of <italic>S</italic><sub>0</sub>(<italic>t</italic>), the fractional single photon response in the absence of feedback regulation: <disp-formula id="eqn17"><label>(A13)</label><tex-math id="M20">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{r_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right }{r_{max}}=I{\cdot}A_{c}{\cdot}{\int _{0}^{t}}S_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right dt{\mathrm{.}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>The steady response (at <italic>t</italic> = &#x0221e;) is <disp-formula id="eqn18"><label>(A14)</label><tex-math id="M21">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\begin{matrix}\frac{r_{0} \left \left({\mathrm{{\infty}}}\right) \right }{r_{max}}=I{\cdot}A_{c}{\cdot}{\int _{0}^{{\mathrm{{\infty}}}}}S_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right dt\\ =I{\cdot}A_{c}{\cdot}S_{m0}{\cdot}t_{i0}{\mathrm{,}}\end{matrix}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <italic>S</italic><sub>m0</sub> is the amplitude, and <italic>t</italic><sub>i0</sub> is the integration time of the normalized single photon response. Combining <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn18">Eqs. A12&#x02013;A14</xref>, <disp-formula id="eqn19"><label>(A15)</label><tex-math id="M22">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\begin{matrix}F \left \left(t\right) \right =\frac{A_{c}}{n_{cG}}{\int _{0}^{t}}S_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right dt\\ =\frac{A_{c}{\cdot}S_{m0}{\cdot}t_{i0}}{n_{cG}}f_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right {\mathrm{,}}\end{matrix}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>and the steady level of <italic>F</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) is <disp-formula id="eqn20"><label>(A16)</label><tex-math id="M23">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}F \left \left({\mathrm{{\infty}}}\right) \right =\frac{A_{c}{\cdot}S_{m0}{\cdot}t_{i0}}{n_{cG}}{\mathrm{.}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>Thus, the sensitivity factor <italic>F</italic> that relates the &#x0201c;no adaptation&#x0201d; step response to stimulus intensity can be obtained from a flash response in the linear range during inhibition of Ca<sup>2+</sup> feedback.</p><p>To estimate <italic>F</italic>, we recorded dim flash responses of individual frog rods in normal Ringer's solution. We multiplied the response integrals by a correction factor derived from the ratio of dim flash response areas recorded in the presence and absence of Ca<sup>2+</sup> feedback inhibition from rods of species more amenable to these sorts of experiments. The effect of inhibiting Ca<sup>2+</sup> feedback on the kinetics and sensitivity of dim flash responses has been studied extensively in the rods of the tiger salamander (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib56">Matthews et al., 1988</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib28">Fain et al., 1989</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib62">Nikonov et al., 1998</xref>) and toads (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib70">Rieke and Baylor, 1996</xref>). The ratio of dim flash response areas recorded in the presence and absence of Ca<sup>2+</sup> feedback inhibition from these studies ranged from 5.7 to 11. We adopted the value of 5.7 from toad because their ROS dimensions and the response properties of their rods in normal Ringer's were most similar to those of bullfrog rods. If instead the value of 11 was selected, then the &#x0201c;no adaptation&#x0201d; response-intensity relation would shift to lower intensities and the magnitude of adaptation would increase 1.9-fold.</p><p>To assess the magnitude of adaptation early in the step response, there is an additional consideration. After a step change in illumination, the overall PDE activity reaches a steady level relatively slowly, due to the slow time constant of PDE shutoff, <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>E</sub></italic>. The term <italic>f</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) from <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn9">Eq. A5</xref> with sufficient accuracy approaches the steady level of 1 for <italic>t</italic> &#x0003e; 3<italic>&#x003c4;<sub>E</sub></italic> (<xref rid="fig7" ref-type="fig">Fig. 7</xref>). In our analysis, <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>R</sub></italic> was assigned the value of 0.48 s (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib62">Nikonov et al., 1998</xref>), and <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>C</sub></italic> was taken as an estimate of <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>E</sub></italic> (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib49">Lyubarsky et al., 1996</xref>). The values we obtained for <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>C</sub></italic> ranged from 2.1 to 3.8 s, the mean value being 3.0 s (<xref ref-type="table" rid="tbl1">Table I</xref>, column 7). However, note that <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>R</sub></italic> and <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>E</sub></italic> enter <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn8">Eqs. A4</xref> and <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn9">A5</xref> symmetrically, so it is not important to which time constant a particular value is assigned. The time courses of <italic>f</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) for <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>E</sub></italic> values of 2, 3, and 4 s are illustrated in <xref rid="fig7" ref-type="fig">Fig. 7</xref> A. Variation in the magnitude of <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>R</sub></italic> had a much smaller effect (<xref rid="fig7" ref-type="fig">Fig. 7</xref> B). This means that the steady level of F(&#x0221e;) = <italic>A</italic><sub>c</sub> &#x000b7; <italic>S<sub>m0</sub></italic>&#x000b7; <italic>t</italic><sub>i0</sub>/<italic>n</italic><sub>cG</sub> can only be used for <italic>t</italic> &#x02265; 9 s. To predict the relation between the no adaptation response and intensity at times earlier than 9 s, it was necessary to estimate the transient term that depends on both <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>E</sub></italic> and <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>R</sub></italic>. For example, at <italic>t</italic> = 2.5 s, <italic>f</italic> = 0.48.</p><p>The ratio of the intensity required to elicit any given fractional response to the intensity required to elicit the same response (leaving the same circulating current) in the absence of adaptation provides a measure of the extent of adaptation. Let us express it in terms of the parameters of the phototransduction cascade. From <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn12">Eq. A8</xref>, the light intensity <italic>I</italic> that results in a certain fixed level of circulating current <italic>i</italic> is <disp-formula id="eqn21"><label>(A17)</label><tex-math id="M24">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}I=\frac{{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{d}}{A_{c}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{{\mathrm{{\infty}}}}{\cdot}f \left \left(t\right) \right }{\cdot} \left \left[\frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}}{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{0}}{\cdot}\frac{K_{cG_{0}}}{K_{cG}}{\cdot} \left \left(\frac{i_{d}}{i}\right) \right ^{{1}/{n_{cG}}}-1\right] \right {\mathrm{,}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <italic>I</italic> is of course a function of time. Thus, <disp-formula id="eqn22"><label>(A18)</label><tex-math id="M25">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\begin{matrix}\frac{I}{I_{0}}= \left \left(\frac{{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{{\mathrm{{\infty}}}0}{\cdot}f_{0} \left \left(t\right) \right }{{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{{\mathrm{{\infty}}}}{\cdot}f \left \left(t\right) \right }\right) \right {\cdot}\\ \frac{ \left \left(\displaystyle\frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}}{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{0}}\right) \right {\cdot} \left \left(\displaystyle\frac{K_{cG_{0}}}{K_{cG}}\right) \right {\cdot} \left \left(\displaystyle\frac{i_{d}}{i}\right) \right ^{{1}/{n_{cG}}}-1}{ \left \left(\displaystyle\frac{i_{d}}{i}\right) \right ^{{1}/{n_{cG}}}-1}\end{matrix}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>Since we are considering intensities resulting in the same response amplitude, <italic>i</italic> = <italic>i</italic><sub>0</sub>. In the steady state, and for bright stimuli where <italic>i</italic><sub>d</sub>/<italic>i</italic> &#x0226b; 1, <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn22">Eq. A18</xref> simplifies to <disp-formula id="eqn23"><label>(A19)</label><tex-math id="M26">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\begin{matrix}\frac{I}{I_{0}}= \left \left(\frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}}{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{0}}\right) \right {\cdot} \left \left(\frac{K_{cG_{0}}}{K_{cG}}\right) \right {\cdot} \left \left(\frac{{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{{\mathrm{{\infty}}}0}}{{\mathrm{{\beta}}}_{{\mathrm{{\infty}}}}}\right) \right \\ = \left \left(\frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}}{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{0}}\right) \right {\cdot}\; \left \left(\frac{K_{cG_{0}}}{K_{cG}}\right) \right {\cdot} \left \left(\frac{{\mathrm{{\nu}}}_{RE_{0}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\tau}}}_{R_{0}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\tau}}}_{E_{0}}{\cdot}k_{cat_{0}}}{{\mathrm{{\nu}}}_{RE}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\tau}}}_{R}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{\tau}}}_{E}{\cdot}k_{cat}}\right) \right {\mathrm{.}}\end{matrix}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>A similar equation was derived by <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib43">Koutalos et al. (1995b)</xref>. <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn23">Eq. A19</xref> gives the extent of adaptation as the product of the degree of guanylate cyclase activation, the degree of modulation of the channel's affinity for cGMP and the modulation of the light-induced cGMP hydrolysis. The latter, in turn, is the product of the modulations of the catalytic activity of R*, R*, and PDE* lifetimes and PDE* catalytic activity proper. The possible contribution of each of these factors to overall light adaptation is evaluated in the <sc>discussion</sc>.</p><p>We can now justify the form of <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn2">Eq. 2</xref> used to characterize the time course of adaptation. Let us consider the Ca<sup>2+</sup> feedback onto guanylate cyclase. From NLP's <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn14">Eq. A10</xref> and <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn20">Eq. A16</xref> of <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib30">Forti et al. (1989)</xref>, <disp-formula id="eqn24"><label>(A20)</label><tex-math id="M27">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\begin{matrix}{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}={\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{min}+\frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{min}+{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{max}}{1+ \left \left({Ca}/{K_{cyc}}\right) \right ^{n_{cyc}}}\\ \approx \frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{max}}{1+ \left \left({Ca}/{K_{cyc}}\right) \right ^{n_{cyc}}}{\mathrm{,}}\end{matrix}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <italic>Ca</italic> is the cytoplasmic Ca<sup>2+</sup> concentration, <italic>K</italic><sub>cyc</sub> is the half-inhibition constant of cyclase, and <italic>n</italic><sub>cyc</sub> is Hill's coefficient for the regulation. <italic>&#x003b1;<sub>min</sub></italic> can be neglected in <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn24">Eq. A20</xref> since <italic>&#x003b1;<sub>max</sub></italic> &#x0226b; <italic>&#x003b1;<sub>min</sub></italic> (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib42">Koutalos et al., 1995a</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib13">Calvert et al., 1998</xref>&#x0003b; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib63">Nikonov et al., 2000</xref>). In darkness, <disp-formula id="eqn25"><label>(A21)</label><tex-math id="M28">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{0}=\frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{max}}{1+ \left \left({Ca_{0}}/{K_{cyc}}\right) \right ^{n_{cyc}}}{\mathrm{.}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>The degree of activation of guanylate cyclase at any given moment is <disp-formula id="eqn26"><label>(A22)</label><tex-math id="M29">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\begin{matrix}A=\frac{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}}{{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}_{0}}=\frac{1+ \left \left({Ca_{0}}/{K_{cyc}}\right) \right ^{n_{cyc}}}{1+ \left \left({Ca \left \left(t\right) \right }/{K_{cyc}}\right) \right ^{n_{cyc}}}\\ =\frac{A_{max}}{1+ \left \left({Ca \left \left(t\right) \right }/{K_{cyc}}\right) \right ^{n_{cyc}}}{\mathrm{.}}\end{matrix}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p><italic>A</italic><sub>max</sub> may be thought of as the degree of suppression of guanylate cyclase activity in darkness. If <italic>Ca</italic> declines in light exponentially, <italic>Ca</italic>(<italic>t</italic>) = <italic>Ca</italic><sub>0</sub> &#x000b7; exp(&#x02212;<italic>t</italic>/&#x003c4;), <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn26">Eq. A22</xref> yields <disp-formula id="eqn27"><label>(A23)</label><tex-math id="M30">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}A \left \left(t\right) \right =\frac{A_{max}}{1+ \left \left(A_{max}-1\right) \right {\cdot}{\mathrm{exp}} \left \left(-t{\cdot}{n_{cyc}}/{{\mathrm{{\tau}}}}\right) \right }{\mathrm{,}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>which is essentially one of the terms in <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn2">Eq. 2</xref>. If there are two independent regulations with magnitudes <italic>A</italic><sub>1</sub> and <italic>A</italic><sub>2</sub> and time constants <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>1</sub></italic> and <italic>&#x003c4;<sub>2</sub></italic>, then the total regulation is given by the product of the two components (<xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn23">Eq. A19</xref>). Thus, we obtain <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eqn2">Eq. 2</xref>. Note that the time constant obtained from the fit is <italic>n</italic><sub>cyc</sub> times faster than the time constant of Ca<sup>2+</sup> decline.</p></app></app-group></back></article>